well and  happy birthday Anna and we heard from  them we've been talking a lot about the  NHS staff and how they really are on the  frontline of the crisis and there's been  reports that they're not having a  protective equipment  they need many doctors writing into the  Sunday Times today saying that they feel  abandoned the chair of the British  Medical Association was on the show last  weekend saying that there's not enough  testing of NHS staff is this something  that you're worried about well I think a  lot has changed in the last week and we  have had lorries on the road pretty much  every day going around the country we  had a very difficult Select Committee  hearing on Tuesday in which we raised  these issues with the Simon Stephens and  the NHS really has moved heaven and  earth to get protective equipment out to  every hospital the concerns that are  being raised are that for treating  coronavirus patients the advice  previously was to use these very high  specification masks the FFP three masks  and the sort of the full gowns hazmat  suits and that has changed to only using  that very high grade equipment when  you're putting on ventilators and so on  so I think the solution to that if it's  possible is to order more of that high  grade equipment more of those grounds  more of the FFP three masks and - one of  those as quickly as possible because  those are clearly the highest grade  equipment and those the things that the  medics would feel most comfortable  wearing and would obviously all like to  give them that degree of comfort but I  do think we need to recognize that a lot  has improved in the last few days and we  haven't got much time left but I do want  to get a quick question in about the  economic package announced by the  government this week lots of help there  for many people across the country of  course but there has been some focus on  the self-employed who would be eligible  for just 94 pounds a week if they are  unable to work is that enough well  clearly 94 pounds a week is not enough  for most people to be able to survive on  but in reality this is the most  comprehensive economic package that has  been announced anywhere in the Western  world and it's a huge step for the  government to do that and it's a vote of  confidence that when we get to the other  side when we put the virus behind us  whenever we're able to do that we will  be able to get back  to normal life and the quickest way to  do that is to avoid having 1 to 3  million people losing their jobs in the  next few months because that would  create a much longer term denting  confidence so I think it's an  unprecedented and very very important  package and I'm sure the chance we'll be  looking at self-employed as well and  doing what we can to support them going  forward
 

